Steven Reeves wrote:
> So, as I play more NetHack, I have more questions as I'm trying to avoid
> reading spoilers.  (You know, maybe I should just give up this "no
> spoiler" weirdness and make it a lot easier on myself.)

As someone else mentioned, there's no turning back.  I would recommend
staying unspoiled as long as you can stand it.

> Of course, in just reading the newsgroup, I learn a few things here and
> there.  Sometimes I watch games online and see people doing odd things.

...Sometimes people do odd things because they're silly.  It's not
ALWAYS going to be something useful.  Especially on NAO.

>  One guy who kept dipping long swords into fountains?  I tried that as
> well and ended up with a rusty longsword, just like the other guy.  I
> have no idea what the point of that exercise is... 

Nethack is filled with actions that appear to "do nothing" unless a
certain set of circumstances are present.

I can think of at least three different sets of circumstances in which
#dipping a longsword into a fountain will produce something other than a
rusty longsword.  But, as you surmised, it may take some experimentation
before you happen upon those circumstances yourself.

> First, at the end of every game (well, at least games where I don't die
> early), I have piles of unidentified wands, potions and scrolls.  I'm
> scared to use any of them randomly because I don't know what they do.

This is both fear, and wisdom.

> I've been told (by people in #nethack, on freenode) that the only way to
> identify them is by a scroll of identify.  

(Or by casting the spell of identify, which one can obtain from the
spellbook of identify.  For most characters, though, the scroll of
identify is the available means.)

>This is all well and good,
> except unless I'm a wizard (that starts off with one such scroll), I
> never come across scrolls of identify.  (Or, if I do, I don't know what
> they are and never use them.)

Identify scrolls are relatively common.  It is likely that you come
across them frequently and just don't know what they are, and so don't
use them.  Figuring out a reliable way to determine what identify
scrolls are for any given game will be a great boon to your future play.

> I find that usually when I die, I had a
> scroll/potion/etc that would have saved me, if only I'd knew what it
> was.  So, is there any other way to identify stuff?  

That depends on what you mean by "identify" stuff.  There are several
different types of "identified" object: an object that is fully
identified, which shows up in your inventory as:

a - a blessed rustproof +3 studded leather armor

...and there's knowing what an object does, even though it's not
properly identified...

a - a studded leather armor

...and finally, there's the knowledge that an object is relatively safe
to use, even though you don't know its full attributes.

a - an uncursed studded leather armor

* Note: "relatively" means just that.  Some things are inherently safer
than others.

There are definitely ways to figure out what certain objects do without
having to formally identify them.

> Or do I just have
> to bite the bullet and start randomly using things and hope they don't
> cause me trouble?  I've noticed, when watching people online at high
> levels, all their stuff in inventory is IDed... so I'm hoping I'm
> missing something here.

Fear not: you are, in fact, missing several tricks.

> anything else.  I'm thinking it's worth it to switch up classes fairly
> often?

Once you've become more experienced at the game and have figured out a
few solid basic strategies, you could switch classes occasionally -- but
I would stick with a single class for quite some time, so you get
familiar with what you have and, more importantly, what you "need to
get".  A great deal of the game is based around determining what, at any
point in time, you "need to get", and different classes "need to get"
different things.

> However, in a tangent to this question, I think the reason why all my
> Monks have died on dlvl 8 is because of my armor situation.  

Well, that's probably not _precisely_ why your monks have died, but the
armor restriction is a non-trivial issue that monks have to figure out a
way to resolve -- especially unspoiled monks.

> I've been
> informed (again by random people in #nethack, who may be wrong or just
> lying for their own amusement) that Monks can't wear any armor except
> gloves, helms and cloaks.  Well, they can, but they suffer penalties. 

This is correct, though the penalties are specifically against "body
armor".  "body armor" does not include gloves, helms, or cloaks, but
_also_ doesn't include a couple other types of armor that are floating
around out there.

> Is
> this right?  I feel like I'm walking around naked out there.  Though, I
> still do well with my Monks... so maybe I'm okay as is.  I wish I could
> put something on my body, though.

Personally, I recommend switching to Valkyrie, Barbarian, or Samurai for
awhile -- the armor issue will become more of an issue the further you
get in the game, and again, is not a trivial problem for someone who is
choosing to avoid spoilers.

> Finally, I have a question about altars.  I've figured out you can
> sacrifice corpses there.  (I'm assuming #offer is the same as
> sacrifice.)  However, sacrificing doesn't seem to accomplish anything.

See above; perhaps there are certain circumstances that also need to be
present before something will happen.

> People say you should... and whenever I'm on a level with an altar of
> the correct alignment, I haul over corpses of things and #offer them
> up... they disappear in a burst of flame (or nothing happens and I still
> have the corpse.)  This seems pointless.  Should I keep doing this or am
> I using altars incorrectly?  

Sacrificing is a valid method of using altars, and can indeed yield you
a positive result.

